From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] A compile does not take away the code it is replacing

open_output_file(recreate) unlinks the old artifact and creates another in its
place, so from the moment dex2oat starts until it finishes, the path holds a
partial file. Measured on the device during a compile of WeRead (2026-10-28,
V91): base.odex growing from zero towards 100.3 MB, and

    dumpsys package dexopt -> arm: invalid[]

for the whole twenty minutes. A process started in that window gets no compiled
code at all and interprets everything, which is slower than never having
compiled. AOSP tolerates this because the job that compiles waits for a device
that is idle *and on a charger*, so the window belongs to nobody; a Kindle is
rarely on a charger, and frameworks/base patch 0066 is about to make that job
run here, which turns the window into the owner's.

It also means an interrupted compile is worse than no compile: the cleanup
lambdas unlink what was half-written, and the application is left with neither
the old code nor the new. JobScheduler cancels this job the moment the owner
picks the device up, so without this patch every cancellation would cost the
application its compiled code until some later run finished -- and a run that
takes twenty minutes on this machine is cancelled easily.

So each artifact is built at <path>.staging and renamed over the one it
replaces once dex2oat has succeeded. The invariant that has to hold is that at
every instant the application has either the code it had before or the code just
built, never neither, and rename() inside a directory is atomic, so it does.

Four details, two of them from a review of the first version of this patch:

  - dex2oat is handed file descriptors (--oat-fd, --output-vdex-fd,
    --app-image-fd), and only --oat-location is a path. That stays the final
    name, so nothing recorded inside the file mentions the staging one.

  - The oat is renamed last. It is what OatFileAssistant reads first, so until
    it appears the old set is whole and in use, and once it appears the new set
    is whole.

  - **What is committed is what this run staged, not what is on the disk.**
    The first version asked the filesystem -- if a staging file was there, it
    was renamed into place. installd killed outright leaves a staging file that
    nobody cleaned up, and a later run does not always stage all three: an app
    image is only built for a profile-guided filter, which after pw3.mk's change
    to pm.dexopt.bg-dexopt is no longer the common case. That run would have
    found the leftover image and published it beside an oat it does not belong
    to. Now the caller says which artifacts it staged, staged_ready() checks
    every one of them before any is renamed, and the two branches of
    maybe_open_app_image that stage nothing unlink the staging path so that a
    leftover cannot survive to be mistaken for this run's work.

  - **The vdex is no longer updated in place, and that path is gone rather than
    disabled.** AOSP took it when three conditions held together: the location
    written is the one read, the reason to compile is a boot image change (so no
    running process can be using the file), and the compilation is not
    profile-guided. It is the one path where a compile that fails or is
    interrupted cannot leave the old artifact intact, because it writes over a
    file with nothing to roll back to -- so the first version of this patch left
    it alone and the invariant above quietly carried an exception the prose did
    not admit to. What in-place saves is writing a second copy of the vdex: at
    most 70 MB here, transiently, against 978 MB free. The invariant is worth
    more than the copy. Removing the branch leaves open_vdex_files'
    profile_guided parameter with no reader, so it goes too; the caller still
    has the value for maybe_open_app_image and the retry.

Each file is flushed before its rename so that a power cut cannot leave the name
pointing at contents that never reached the disk. The directory is deliberately
not flushed after: a rename lost to a power cut leaves the old artifact in
place, which is the right way for this to fail. What remains is a rename failing
on a filesystem that has stopped taking writes, which can leave the set mixed;
ART answers a mismatched oat and vdex with kOatDexOutOfDate, so the application
interprets until something compiles it again rather than failing to start.

Not run on the device yet when this patch was written. It has been built: the
version before this one did not compile (`unused parameter 'profile_guided'`,
-Werror), which is what sent the in-place branch from disabled to deleted.

